course: money
lesson: 30
title: Why some jobs pay more
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Pay is not a score for how good or important a person is; it mostly depends on how many people can do a job and how much others want that job done.
explain: A doctor trains for 5 or 6 years at university and more after, and few people can do the work, so doctors earn more; lots of people can learn to clean in a few weeks, so cleaning pays less, though it matters just as much.
explain: Picture a football stadium with 60,000 fans and millions more watching on TV: that money flows to the few stars everyone wants to see, so a top player might earn £100,000 a week, which is £5,200,000 a year.
explain: Other things push pay up too: danger, like work on an oil rig; expensive places, like London; extra training; and more responsibility; and some things should never change pay, like whether you are a man or a woman.
explain: Many people feel it is unfair that a nurse who saves lives earns far less than a footballer; others say pay follows what people choose to spend on; both sides have reasons, and the argument goes on.
fact: Jobs that need long training and rare skills usually pay more, because fewer people can do them and they are in demand.
fact: Supply and demand means how many people can do a job compared with how much people want it done; when lots of people can do a job, pay tends to be lower.
fact: Pay is not a measure of how important a job is: a cleaner on £22,000 and a programmer on £50,000 are £28,000 apart, but both jobs matter.
fact: A top footballer might earn £100,000 a week, which is 52 × £100,000 = £5,200,000 a year, because millions pay to watch and buy shirts.
fact: A nurse earning about £35,000 a year needs about 3 years to earn £100,000, which that footballer earns in a single week.
fact: Jobs in expensive cities often pay more; London weighting is extra pay for working in London, like £35,000 instead of £28,000, which is £7,000 more.
fact: A qualification is a certificate showing you have learned something; training at night college took one dad from £22,000 to £30,000, which is £8,000 more a year.
fact: Equal pay laws in many countries, including the UK, say men and women must get the same pay for the same work; sometimes it still does not happen, and people fight to fix it.
fact: A CEO is the top boss of a company; a CEO on £2,000,000 a year earns 100 times as much as a worker on £20,000; some say bosses earn it, others say it is unfair.
fact: Dangerous jobs often pay more to get people to do them, like £60,000 on an oil rig instead of £35,000 on land; this is sometimes called danger money.
fact: Pay depends on skills, hard work, chances, where you live and luck, and money is not a measure of how good a person is.
exercise: Why do doctors usually earn more than shop workers? | They train for years and fewer people can do the job | Shop work is a much less important job for the whole country | Doctors are paid by their patients in cash | Doctors work fewer hours than shop workers
exercise: About how long does a doctor train at university? | About 5 to 6 years, then more after | About 5 to 6 weeks, then nothing after | About 1 year in total | About 30 years before working
exercise: A footballer earns £100,000 a week. How much is that in 52 weeks? | £5,200,000 | £520,000 | £1,000,000 | £52,000,000
exercise: Why do top footballers earn so much? | Millions pay to watch them and buy their shirts | The government pays all footballers the same | Football is the most dangerous job in the world | Footballers train longer than doctors do
exercise: A nurse earns about £35,000 a year. Roughly how many years to earn £100,000? | About 3 years | About 30 years | About 10 years | About 1 year
exercise: A programmer earns £50,000 and a cleaner £22,000. What is the difference? | £28,000 | £72,000 | £32,000 | £22,000
exercise: Ella asks if cleaning is less important because it pays less. What is true? | No, more people can do it, so it is paid less | Yes, because pay always shows how important a job is | Yes, because cleaners chose to be paid less for it | No, because cleaners earn more than most programmers
exercise: Mo's uncle earns £35,000 in London and his friend £28,000 for the same job in a small town. How much more in London? | £7,000 | £63,000 | £3,000 | £17,000
exercise: Why do jobs in London often pay more? | Living there, like rent, costs more | London jobs are always harder than others | The King pays London workers extra | Workers in London work twice as many hours
exercise: Finn's dad went from £22,000 to £30,000 after college. How much more a year? | £8,000 | £52,000 | £12,000 | £7,000
exercise: Aisha's uncle earns £30,000 and her aunt £27,000 for the same job. What is the difference, and is it allowed? | £3,000, and equal pay laws say it shouldn't happen | £3,000, and it is fine because he is a man | £57,000, and it is fine if they are married | £3,000, and only the aunt can complain to the boss
exercise: A boss earns £2,000,000 and a worker £20,000. How many times more does the boss earn? | 100 times | 10 times | 1,000 times | 50 times
exercise: Tom's dad grumbles about the boss's pay and Tom's mum says, "Some say he earns it." What is fair to say? | Some say bosses earn it, others say it is too much | Everyone agrees that bosses actually earn far too little | Everyone agrees that bosses earn exactly the right amount | Nobody has any opinion at all about how much bosses earn
exercise: An oil rig worker earns £60,000 and a similar job on land pays £35,000. Why the extra? | The work is dangerous and hard, so it pays more | Oil rigs are much closer to the shops than jobs on land | Oil rig workers have to pay for the oil they take out | Jobs at sea are always easier than jobs back on land
exercise: Ellie asks if being clever makes you rich. What did Grandad say? | Cleverness helps, but hard work and luck matter too | Only clever people ever manage to get any money at all | Clever people are always the poorest people in the country | Money depends only on which school you happened to go to
test: Your mate goes, "Cleaners get paid less cos cleaning doesn't matter, innit." What's the best reply? | It matters a lot; more people can do it, so it's paid less | He's right, low pay shows a job doesn't matter | Cleaners choose to earn less to be kind | Cleaners actually earn the most of anyone
test: A nurse from Belfast says, "We save lives, and yer man on the telly earns in a week what I earn in years." ("Yer man" means "that fella".) What's true? | Pay doesn't always match how useful a job is, and people argue about it | Nurses earn more than footballers every year | Footballers save more lives than nurses do | It's a lie; footballers and nurses earn the same
test: Josh says, "Footballers get paid way too much." His mate says, "But millions watch 'em!" What's going on? | They have different views; both point to real reasons | Josh is right and nobody watches football | His mate is right, and nobody at all thinks it's too much money | Neither, because footballers aren't paid at all
test: What would happen to a cleaner's pay if suddenly very few people could do cleaning? | It would probably go up, because cleaners would be in demand | It would go down, because fewer cleaners means less work | Nothing, pay is set once and never changes | Cleaners would stop being paid at all
test: Mo's uncle, a Londoner, says, "Same job, bruv, but London rent is bare mental, so they pay more." ("Bare" means very.) His pay is £35,000 and out of town it's £28,000. What's the extra for? | The higher cost of living in London; it's £7,000 more | Working harder than people in other towns do; it's £63,000 | Being from London by birth; it's £7,000 | Nothing, it's a mistake on his payslip
test: Ellie's grandad says, "Loads of rich folk aren't that clever, ha." What lesson is he giving? | Pay depends on many things, including luck, not just cleverness | Clever people are never rich at all | Being rich means you must be very clever | Money shows exactly how clever someone is
test: Aisha's aunt and uncle do the same job. "Yer man gets three grand more cos he's a fella," jokes the aunt. ("Grand" means a thousand pounds.) What's the truth? | That shouldn't happen; equal pay laws say same work, same pay | That's fair, men are always meant to earn more | That's fine as long as she gets a nicer desk | It's normal, the law says men earn more
test: A cartoon shows a doctor and a cleaner on exactly the same pay because "all jobs are equally hard". What's the real reason pay differs? | Training, how many can do the job, and demand differ | Real doctors are actually paid less than most cleaners are | Pay depends only on how hard and tiring the job is each day | Everyone in real life earns exactly the same amount of pay
test: Finn's dad: "Two years of night college — no, two and a half — and now I earn more." He went from £22,000 to £30,000. How much more each year? | £8,000 | £52,000 | £10,000 | £2,500
test: A boss earns £2 million and a worker £20,000. A Gen Z intern goes, "That's not it, that's lowkey wild." ("Lowkey" means quietly, kind of.) What's the honest summary? | The boss earns 100 times more; some say it's fair, some say it's too much | The boss earns 2 times more and everyone agrees it's fair | The boss earns 10 times more and it's against the law | The worker earns more than the boss
test: Zac says, "Dangerous jobs pay more so people will do them." Which job most likely pays danger money? | Working on an oil rig at sea | Reading books in a quiet library | Selling ice cream on a sunny beach | Sorting post in a quiet office
exam: It is given that a professional footballer receives £100,000 per week. Determine the remuneration received over fifty-two weeks. | £5,200,000 | £520,000 | £1,000,000 | £52,000,000
exam: Ascertain how many times the remuneration of a chief executive in receipt of £2,000,000 exceeds that of an employee in receipt of £20,000. | 100 | 10 | 1,000 | 50
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the principal reason for which certain occupations attract greater remuneration? | The scarcity of persons able to perform them relative to the demand for their performance | The greater moral worth of persons engaged in such occupations | A statutory scale under which all occupations are ranked by importance | The age of the persons engaged in such occupations
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning equal pay in the United Kingdom. | The law requires that men and women receive equal pay for equal work | The law permits employers to remunerate men more highly than women | The law requires that all employees receive identical remuneration | The law makes no provision whatsoever in respect of pay
